{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1535107-awakened-ghouls","id":1535107,"title":"Awakened Ghouls","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2024-05-03T10:28:03-07:00","id":1535107,"title":"Awakened Ghouls","content":"The V20 Black Hand book mentions that Itrajana using Magik to copy classical vampire powers are considred to cast Subtle effects. Would that be the same for Awakened Ghouls using modern Sphere magik rather than the Pillars of the Itarajana?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>When will you rage?","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1405-anteros","name":"Anteros","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1405_4.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"661"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-03T11:09:23-07:00","id":1535109,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Anteros</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1535107-awakened-ghouls#post1535107\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The V20 Black Hand book mentions that Itrajana using Magik to copy classical vampire powers are considred to cast Subtle effects. Would that be the same for Awakened Ghouls using modern Sphere magik rather than the Pillars of the Itarajana?</div> </div> </div> </div>RAW yes (see book of secrets)<br /> But i think thats a bit too op and dont allow it in my games.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28439-quelsec","name":"quelsec","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"407"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-03T13:37:41-07:00","id":1535111,"title":"","content":"Being Awakened and being a Ghoul are said (by the line editors) to counteract each other. Ghouldom is a state of Slavery. Being an Awakened Mage is about freedom and responsibility. They work against each other.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-03T18:26:19-07:00","id":1535120,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Astromancer</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1535107-awakened-ghouls?p=1535111#post1535111\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Being Awakened and being a Ghoul are said (by the line editors) to counteract each other. Ghouldom is a state of Slavery. Being an Awakened Mage is about freedom and responsibility. They work against each other.</div> </div> </div> </div>Not quite that.<br /> <br /> The fluff about Ghouldom in a Mage is meant to be a prompt for personal conflict and angst, never to be an outright prohibition.<br /> <br /> It only goes as far as to suggest that at some point the Mage won't increase in Arete anymore, and even that is only stated in some few books.<br /> <br /> On M20 being a Ghoul is a Merit. <b><i>Book of Secrets</i></b> pp 75.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-04T00:06:24-07:00","id":1535131,"title":"","content":"M20 has lately ditched the \u201cif you're affiliated with [insert other supernatural type], then you can do Magick that mimics what they con do, and it will be Coincidental\u201d notion. Look up M20's versions of the Merits for Werewolf and Changeling, traits that in their earlier incarnations have access to Werewolf-like and Changeling-like Magick as Coincidental. The closest you get to that is when such Merits let you learn the actual supernatural powers of another type, such as how being a Ghoul lets you pick up one or two (?) Disciplines. Those Disciplines <i>aren't </i>Magick, and don't generate Paradox the way Magick does. But even there, the ghouled mage doesn't get to emulate a Discipline using Magick and have it automatically be Coincidental. <br /> <br /> With that said, there's a Storyteller's Vault product (<a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/417652\" target=\"_blank\">Foreign Magicks</a>) that reworks the \u201ccrossover Merits\u201d (Ghoul, Kinfolk, Medium, and Fae-Blooded) to provide access to Minor Spheres (as described in <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/388362\" target=\"_blank\">Book of Fragments</a>, mostly) that emulate metaphysics of the respective crossover lines. For Awakened Ghouls, that replaces the access to Disciplines with access to the Minor Spheres of Blood, Immortality, and Dominance. This still doesn't give you a Coincidental edge when working with those Minor Spheres; that's still determined entirely by the Reality Zone that you're in.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-04T09:13:46-07:00","id":1535142,"title":"","content":"I wouldn't say it ditched. In fact, it doesn't even say you have to be a &quot;half-thing&quot; to access such benefits. The core doesn't suggest that Ghouls can emulate Disciplines with Coincidental Magicka, it outright says that any Mage can do so on most places due to pop culture.<br /> <br /> The greatest benefit of learning actual static powers isn't to use them as Coincidental (although that's certainly a thing), but to access mechanics that give you more oomph on an impromptu use than rolling Arete.<br /> <br /> While I am of the thought that mimicking vulgar powers as Coincidental because pop culture is silly, RAW does suggest that, and regardless of mystical ties.<br /> <br /> So... yeah, if OP wants to roll with that, this is a fair use of the rules.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-05T02:28:54-07:00","id":1535165,"title":"","content":"The Fatal Addiction is very dangerous to your enlightenment, but as others have said it doesn't stop you from being a Mage. It will be a pretty significant barrier to you Ascending, and &quot;letting go of the weird vampire blood addiction&quot; feels like pretty obvious self-improvement fodder to form part of a Seeking. There have been writeups where it's basicaly poison to mages, and there's an optional rule in M20 where that is still the case. V20 has it so that Ghoul Mages undergo a &quot;blood fast&quot; before attempting a Seeking to prepare themselves, which comes with risks of aging and so on!<br /> <br /> It's also worth noting, however, that not all Mages are undergoing self improvement, as it were. Some are becoming worse and worse people with each Seeking! High Arete doesn't make you a good or wise person, per se, given that good and wise are both subjective.<br /> <br /> Still, even with a very caustic and power-hungry Paradigm/Avatar, ultimately Ghouldom is a distraction from Ascension. It is stasis. It is ceding control. It is being a puppet. It is being a leech. On a metaphysical and behavioural level you're getting drawn away from the path of Ascension.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-05T18:46:02-07:00","id":1535187,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>11twiggins</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1535107-awakened-ghouls?p=1535165#post1535165\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The Fatal Addiction is very dangerous to your enlightenment, but as others have said it doesn't stop you from being a Mage. It will be a pretty significant barrier to you Ascending, and &quot;letting go of the weird vampire blood addiction&quot; feels like pretty obvious self-improvement fodder to form part of a Seeking. There have been writeups where it's basicaly poison to mages, and there's an optional rule in M20 where that is still the case. V20 has it so that Ghoul Mages undergo a &quot;blood fast&quot; before attempting a Seeking to prepare themselves, which comes with risks of aging and so on!<br /> <br /> It's also worth noting, however, that not all Mages are undergoing self improvement, as it were. Some are becoming worse and worse people with each Seeking! High Arete doesn't make you a good or wise person, per se, given that good and wise are both subjective.<br /> <br /> Still, even with a very caustic and power-hungry Paradigm/Avatar, ultimately Ghouldom is a distraction from Ascension. It is stasis. It is ceding control. It is being a puppet. It is being a leech. On a metaphysical and behavioural level you're getting drawn away from the path of Ascension.</div> </div> </div> </div>While I get the point, I don't completely agree. I think this is one of the worst cases of &quot;mixing with Vampire can only be bad, because reasons&quot;.<br /> <br /> Mage is about belief, it is about changing the world around you to fit the world within. It isn't about being pure and wise so much as it is about defining what you believe to be pure and wise, and then making it real.<br /> <br /> I won't keep a particular vision of what Ghouldom is in the path of any character concept for Mage.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-05T19:46:01-07:00","id":1535188,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>11twiggins</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1535107-awakened-ghouls?p=1535165#post1535165\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The Fatal Addiction is very dangerous to your enlightenment, but as others have said it doesn't stop you from being a Mage. It will be a pretty significant barrier to you Ascending, and &quot;letting go of the weird vampire blood addiction&quot; feels like pretty obvious self-improvement fodder to form part of a Seeking. There have been writeups where it's basicaly poison to mages, and there's an optional rule in M20 where that is still the case. V20 has it so that Ghoul Mages undergo a &quot;blood fast&quot; before attempting a Seeking to prepare themselves, which comes with risks of aging and so on!<br /> <br /> It's also worth noting, however, that not all Mages are undergoing self improvement, as it were. Some are becoming worse and worse people with each Seeking! High Arete doesn't make you a good or wise person, per se, given that good and wise are both subjective.<br /> <br /> Still, even with a very caustic and power-hungry Paradigm/Avatar, ultimately Ghouldom is a distraction from Ascension. It is stasis. It is ceding control. It is being a puppet. It is being a leech. On a metaphysical and behavioural level you're getting drawn away from the path of Ascension.</div> </div> </div> </div>The &quot;Fatal Addiction&quot; was present in only a single book at the time of the &quot;Time of Judgement&quot; line, and was never again re used anywhere else.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28035-magus-maximus","name":"Magus Maximus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"569"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-06T05:08:51-07:00","id":1535191,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>11twiggins</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1535107-awakened-ghouls?p=1535165#post1535165\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The Fatal Addiction is very dangerous to your enlightenment, but as others have said it doesn't stop you from being a Mage. It will be a pretty significant barrier to you Ascending, and &quot;letting go of the weird vampire blood addiction&quot; feels like pretty obvious self-improvement fodder to form part of a Seeking. There have been writeups where it's basicaly poison to mages, and there's an optional rule in M20 where that is still the case. V20 has it so that Ghoul Mages undergo a &quot;blood fast&quot; before attempting a Seeking to prepare themselves, which comes with risks of aging and so on!<br /> <br /> It's also worth noting, however, that not all Mages are undergoing self improvement, as it were. Some are becoming worse and worse people with each Seeking! High Arete doesn't make you a good or wise person, per se, given that good and wise are both subjective.<br /> <br /> Still, even with a very caustic and power-hungry Paradigm/Avatar, ultimately Ghouldom is a distraction from Ascension. It is stasis. It is ceding control. It is being a puppet. It is being a leech. On a metaphysical and behavioural level you're getting drawn away from the path of Ascension.</div> </div> </div> </div>It's in the 30 years since Mage was first published, I have yet to see a single effect that alters morality. There's brainwashing, yes. But I have never seen anyone claim to have magic that makes something that was immoral suddenly be moral.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-06T12:27:06-07:00","id":1535199,"title":"","content":"It makes a certain thematic sense that ghouldom and magedom would not work well together, but there's no reason they should be mutually exclusive either. Nor should the problems only go one way. Just as a mage would be wise not to trust the gifts of a vampire, a vampire would be wise not to let a mage meddle with their blood.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-06T20:04:19-07:00","id":1535206,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1535107-awakened-ghouls?p=1535191#post1535191\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> It's in the 30 years since Mage was first published, I have yet to see a single effect that alters morality. There's brainwashing, yes. But I have never seen anyone claim to have magic that makes something that was immoral suddenly be moral.</div> </div> </div> </div>Funny how morality seems to exist above and beyond the &quot;Demiurge&quot;","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28035-magus-maximus","name":"Magus Maximus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"569"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-07T06:29:47-07:00","id":1535214,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Magus Maximus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1535107-awakened-ghouls?p=1535206#post1535206\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Funny how morality seems to exist above and beyond the &quot;Demiurge&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>It's a shared media written by human writers, but the morality comes from outside. It's always going to look a bit gnostic whether it is intended to or not. For that matter, all shared media will look gnostic because any divinity or metaphysics within the media was actually created by something outside the media.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-07T21:22:24-07:00","id":1535248,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1535107-awakened-ghouls?p=1535214#post1535214\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> It's a shared media written by human writers, but the morality comes from outside. It's always going to look a bit gnostic whether it is intended to or not. For that matter, all shared media will look gnostic because any divinity or metaphysics within the media was actually created by something outside the media.</div> </div> </div> </div>It's an old theological consideration in fact. &quot;God&quot; (if exists) cannot be the source of morality, otherwise anything that God ordered would be automatically good and moral. Therefore, morality is something that God possess, but he is not the source of it. Morality must therefore exist beyond God.<br /> <br /> For an atheist (like me) that may seem like some useless conversation, but it is actually a deep philosophy. It means that there's some sort of &quot;natural morality&quot;. In fact, that's what I believe, since I'm also a Libertarian, I believe in a &quot;natural law&quot; and &quot;natural rights&quot;.<br /> <br /> So yeah, Mages can't use Magik to change morality because morality seems to exist in a &quot;hyper reality&quot; that exists beyond and above the rules of Magik.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28035-magus-maximus","name":"Magus Maximus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"569"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-07T23:16:15-07:00","id":1535252,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Magus Maximus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1535107-awakened-ghouls?p=1535248#post1535248\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> It's an old theological consideration in fact. &quot;God&quot; (if exists) cannot be the source of morality, otherwise anything that God ordered would be automatically good and moral. Therefore, morality is something that God possess, but he is not the source of it. Morality must therefore exist beyond God.<br /> <br /> For an atheist (like me) that may seem like some useless conversation, but it is actually a deep philosophy. It means that there's some sort of &quot;natural morality&quot;. In fact, that's what I believe, since I'm also a Libertarian, I believe in a &quot;natural law&quot; and &quot;natural rights&quot;.<br /> <br /> So yeah, Mages can't use Magik to change morality because morality seems to exist in a &quot;hyper reality&quot; that exists beyond and above the rules of Magik.</div> </div> </div> </div>Dont want to make this a religion talk but if we look at this acording to Jung then morality comes from god he just discarded his anima and was not self aware. He became self aware by interacting with humans in the old testament.<br /> As for changing morality we have to ask what morality is.<br /> If its a universal truth then it can be changed with consensus.<br /> If its a cultural thing? Then it can be changed with culture.<br /> If its a subjective thing then it can be changed manipulating personality.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28439-quelsec","name":"quelsec","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"407"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-08T02:09:05-07:00","id":1535254,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>quelsec</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">If its a universal truth then it can be changed with consensus.</div> </div> </div> </div>Not necessarily. There are \u201cuniversal truths\u201d that the Consensus can't change; they're called \u201cEarthly Foundations\u201d. The thing that often gets overlooked when discussing Earthly Foundations is that such \u201cuniversal truths\u201d <i>can</i> be changed, by a mage who has sufficient dots in the right Sphere. All that the Consensus can do is to decide whether or not the mage will be slammed with Paradox if he changes it; and even then, only if \u201cit\u201d isn't an Earthly Foundation.<br /> <br /> The only thing, besides insufficient Spheres, that restricts what a mage can do is <i>his own </i>beliefs: if a mage doesn't think he can do it, he can't. <br /> <br /> Except, it seems, for morality. Again, in 30 years of publication, I have never once seen an Effect that changes morality. What's right and what's wrong is a metric that mages often <i>disagree on; </i>but it's one of the very few things in the setting that no one <i>changes</i>. The Technocracy, for instance, doesn't go around Terranorming reality into one where they're <i>morally in the right</i>. <br /> <br /> The closest I've ever seen to such a thing even being <i>contemplated</i> was the Tenth Sphere of Telos in <b>Ascension</b>; and note that that Sphere was name-checked, but not detailed. And rather than being something that mages can harness and use to their own ends, it was presented as something that shows up and <i>passes judgment on everyone</i> at the end of all things, not unlike a Supreme Being.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-08T07:33:09-07:00","id":1535262,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1535107-awakened-ghouls?p=1535254#post1535254\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Not necessarily. There are \u201cuniversal truths\u201d that the Consensus can't change; they're called \u201cEarthly Foundations\u201d. The thing that often gets overlooked when discussing Earthly Foundations is that such \u201cuniversal truths\u201d <i>can</i> be changed, by a mage who has sufficient dots in the right Sphere. All that the Consensus can do is to decide whether or not the mage will be slammed with Paradox if he changes it; and even then, only if \u201cit\u201d isn't an Earthly Foundation.<br /> <br /> The only thing, besides insufficient Spheres, that restricts what a mage can do is <i>his own </i>beliefs: if a mage doesn't think he can do it, he can't.<br /> <br /> Except, it seems, for morality. Again, in 30 years of publication, I have never once seen an Effect that changes morality. What's right and what's wrong is a metric that mages often <i>disagree on; </i>but it's one of the very few things in the setting that no one <i>changes</i>. The Technocracy, for instance, doesn't go around Terranorming reality into one where they're <i>morally in the right</i>.<br /> <br /> The closest I've ever seen to such a thing even being <i>contemplated</i> was the Tenth Sphere of Telos in <b>Ascension</b>; and note that that Sphere was name-checked, but not detailed. And rather than being something that mages can harness and use to their own ends, it was presented as something that shows up and <i>passes judgment on everyone</i> at the end of all things, not unlike a Supreme Being.</div> </div> </div> </div>Further, nobody in Mage acts like they can do this. That is, no one actually takes the opinion that they are currently morally wrong and unjustified in their actions, but they can make themselves morally right with a spell. Possibly that might be a Nephandic motivation somewhere, but it's hard to tell with those guys.<br /> <br /> And with morality it is hard to see how a mage could formulate the spell without that opinion. In the same way that if a mage doesn't think he can change something, he can't make a change, if a mage doesn't think there's anything to change, he can't make a change. <br /> <br /> Which shows the real problem with the spell. If a mage thinks he is wrong, he can't cast it, and if a mage thinks he isn't wrong, he also can't cast it.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-08T08:05:11-07:00","id":1535268,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1535107-awakened-ghouls?p=1535254#post1535254\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Not necessarily. There are \u201cuniversal truths\u201d that the Consensus can't change; they're called \u201cEarthly Foundations\u201d. The thing that often gets overlooked when discussing Earthly Foundations is that such \u201cuniversal truths\u201d <i>can</i> be changed, by a mage who has sufficient dots in the right Sphere. All that the Consensus can do is to decide whether or not the mage will be slammed with Paradox if he changes it; and even then, only if \u201cit\u201d isn't an Earthly Foundation.<br /> <br /> The only thing, besides insufficient Spheres, that restricts what a mage can do is <i>his own </i>beliefs: if a mage doesn't think he can do it, he can't.<br /> <br /> Except, it seems, for morality. Again, in 30 years of publication, I have never once seen an Effect that changes morality. What's right and what's wrong is a metric that mages often <i>disagree on; </i>but it's one of the very few things in the setting that no one <i>changes</i>. The Technocracy, for instance, doesn't go around Terranorming reality into one where they're <i>morally in the right</i>.<br /> <br /> The closest I've ever seen to such a thing even being <i>contemplated</i> was the Tenth Sphere of Telos in <b>Ascension</b>; and note that that Sphere was name-checked, but not detailed. And rather than being something that mages can harness and use to their own ends, it was presented as something that shows up and <i>passes judgment on everyone</i> at the end of all things, not unlike a Supreme Being.</div> </div> </div> </div>Exactly. Morality is not just a mere &quot;Earthly Foundation&quot;, but rather something that goes beyond and above that.<br /> <br /> Like the early Christians theologists found out, morality can't possibly come from God, but it's rather an attribute that God possess. Otherwise, if God asked humans to sacrifice children, that would be automatically good and moral, because &quot;morality&quot; would just be whatever random commands God gave.<br /> <br /> Likewise in the game, morality can't just be some &quot;random&quot; &quot;Earthly Foundation&quot;, decided by some Demiurge or by the collective will of all spirits or whatever, but it's rather something above and beyond that","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28035-magus-maximus","name":"Magus Maximus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"569"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-08T08:25:32-07:00","id":1535270,"title":"","content":"All that being said thou, I think that it's important to say that from my atheistic mechanicist point of view, I don't personally believe that morality is this ethereal thing that is a law of the cosmos. I don't believe in morality in the natural world, stars don't have morality (since they don't have agency), a black hole simply pulls things towards it because that's what gravity does, and living beings are just self replicating drones naturally produced from billions of years of chemical reactions. We are self replicating drones. And all that matters is that our genes can continue to exist; we as individuals are just the mere robots that our genes use to perpetuate their existence.<br /> <br /> &quot;Morality&quot; is thus just an emergent pattern of our existence as social animals. We have a better chance at survival by cooperating with one another, rather than living as lonely polar bears. Besides, my genes are also present in my children, in my siblings and even on some of my kin, so I as an individual, I'm an &quot;expendable robot&quot;, all that matters are my genes, but they are perfectly fine in existing on me or on other robots - which is we humans can act &quot;altruistically&quot; towards other humans. It's the selfishness of our genes in action.<br /> <br /> If we were highly intelligent ants, then it would be perfectly &quot;moral&quot; and &quot;good&quot; to sacrifice millions of &quot;drone workers&quot; for the &quot;good of the hive&quot;. Good thing we evolved the way we did then, and I'm glad that I'm a human rather than an insect.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28035-magus-maximus","name":"Magus Maximus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"569"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-08T09:39:48-07:00","id":1535278,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Magus Maximus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1535107-awakened-ghouls?p=1535268#post1535268\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Exactly. Morality is not just a mere &quot;Earthly Foundation&quot;, but rather something that goes beyond and above that.<br /> <br /> Like the early Christians theologists found out, morality can't possibly come from God, but it's rather an attribute that God possess. Otherwise, if God asked humans to sacrifice children, that would be automatically good and moral, because &quot;morality&quot; would just be whatever random commands God gave.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>Close. They didn't find that morality was something outside and above God, but rather that morality comes from Him because it is part of His Nature. Your point remains, though, that it is quite possible to view objective morality as something that comes from outside of creation. Indeed, that is the assumption people tend to make whether they intend to or not.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-08T20:07:14-07:00","id":1535323,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1535107-awakened-ghouls?p=1535278#post1535278\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Close. They didn't find that morality was something outside and above God, but rather that morality comes from Him because it is part of His Nature. Your point remains, though, that it is quite possible to view objective morality as something that comes from outside of creation. Indeed, that is the assumption people tend to make whether they intend to or not.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah. It's an attribute of God, something that he possess, but not something that he creates.<br /> <br /> That's also valid for Mage. And as Dataweaver has already pointed out, it would be a &quot;philosophical paradox&quot; (not to confuse with a &quot;magical Paradox) to have a Magik turn something immoral into something moral or vice versa, because Mages can only ever do what they believe they can do, therefore it would be impossible for a Mage to make something that he believes to be immoral into something moral or vice versa, regardless of what the source of morality could be in the game.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28035-magus-maximus","name":"Magus Maximus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"569"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-14T07:17:59-07:00","id":1535564,"title":"","content":"I disagree with the idea that mage posits moral as something above change<br /> <br /> A completely opposite way of viewing this, and at least equally valid, it's that morality could be little more than an opinion (an intersubjective valuation) that has no real existence outside of &quot;belief&quot;. Thus mages don't change it not because they can't but because it would be pointless: Mages don't cast spells to change their opinion about anything , not just about moral things. There's no magick in the books to change your mind about anything, moral or otherwise<br /> <br /> Let's say that you want to take a walk: You can go trough road A or road B. Neither it's a particularly moral or immoral choice.<br /> <br /> How do you alter your choise between A or B trough magick?. You could scan the paths to see what one it's better, but that's not directly &quot;altering your choice&quot;, rather it's providing you with reasons to change your own opinion.<br /> <br /> How do you change &quot;what you want to do&quot; with magick?. You don't, because the moment you want to do &quot;this&quot;, you've already changed your mind <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> But you can totally change the oppinions, including the whole moral compass of others, with magick. Technocrats do that all the time with &quot;room 101&quot;. How Traditionalist turn traitor against their own people, even close people, it's a strong sign of their morality having changed quite a bit.<br /> <br /> Similarly you can &quot;change your mind&quot; with magick while resisting an external influence. I.E:: Probably a bonded ghoul-mage believes that his domitor it's a great person, if not always a good one. Love often changes people's wordviews, including morality. It could be that the mage now sees something (like, say, the importance of hierarchy or nobility in the case of Ventrue) in a different ligth compared to before the Bonding, and copious positive interactions with &quot;the loved one&quot;. But after a moment of abuse the mage could change his own mind a little, enough to cast a spell to brek &quot;the bond&quot; maybe - and then I imagine the vampire, and the actions the mage realized for it, are going to be seen in a very different ligth. A 100% tunarround of worldview about some beliefs that would be important for both of you it's not something I would discard<br /> <br /> &quot;But you're not directly changing your worldview with magick&quot;. No, because it would be pointless, the moment you want to do that it's already changed. But you're still totally changing your morality with magick, and you're resisting it changing away again when the effect of the bond resumes. To not see these changes as &quot;magical&quot; would be pushing it. No?. Even if you're not directly casting &quot;I will change my own opinions&quot;<br /> <br /> Now, you could argue that there's an objective morality because the authors take these kind of actions as &quot;bad&quot;. But I would say that's still just an intersubjective valuation, even if it's one endorsed by the authors.<br /> The authors are people influenced by the medium like anyone else, they often change their minds about how bad X it's (just see how mage itself has changed between editions...) and I wouldn't adscribe them anything close to a &quot;superior&quot; moral. <br /> <br /> If we started to talk about what the &quot;worst&quot; actions of the technocracy are, and what are justified. I assure you that in many points we will disagree - if morality it's an objective rule of the game, there should be a clear answer about these things, but there's hardly a clear answer. Objective moral, as far as it exists in mage, it's just when the authors are sure something it's bad. And they're not reliable.<br /> <br /> So, I would poisit one can't prove that morality exists as it's own, unchangeable, thing, &quot;outside of magick&quot;. Albeit, of course, it could exist. (important distinction so choristers and others aren't instantly wrong :P)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 05-14-2024, 10:22 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-14T07:41:47-07:00","id":1535566,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> if morality is an objective rule of the game, there should be a clear answer about these things </div> </div> </div>Should there? The claim is that morality is something that Magick can't change, not that it's something that is an objective rule of the game. I agree that different factions disagree on what's moral and what isn't; that's at the heart of the Ascension War. But by that same token, if it was possible to actually alter morality using Magick, the Ascension War would have ended the moment one faction Magically imposed their morality on everyone else. The Traditions would be fundamentally wrong to oppose the Technocracy, because the Union would long since have altered reality so that their methods are moral and opposition to them is immoral. <br /> <br /> Basically, there are two notions of \u201csubjective\u201d at work here, and they conflict. The first is \u201csubjective\u201d in the sense of \u201cwe know what the rules are, but we can change them\u201d; the second is \u201cwe don't even know what the rules are\u201d. Heck, it's entirely possible that the reason why nobody has ever cast a spell to change morality is because there <i>is </i>no such thing as morality to change. But I'm not sure I'd ascribe <i>that </i>view even to the <i>Nephandi. </i>Not as something that they actually <i>believe. </i>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-14T09:14:33-07:00","id":1535569,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1535107-awakened-ghouls?p=1535566#post1535566\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Should there? The claim is that morality is something that Magick can't change, not that it's something that is an objective rule of the game.</div> </div> </div> </div> Well, it has to be something that can't be changed by magick. I.E: The mind can be changed with magick, so morality has to have an existance outside the mind. It could be society, but then you would need to argue why society can't bechanged with magick<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The first is \u201csubjective\u201d in the sense of \u201cwe know what the rules are, but we can change them\u201d; the second is \u201cwe don't even know what the rules are\u201d. </div> </div> </div><b>What are</b> <b>we? </b>Any level of subjectivity implies that the rules are creted and can be changed by the subject, If <b>we </b>it's the level of subjectivity, and <b>we </b>don't know what the rules are, that assumes there are moral rules outside of that level of subjectivity, and thus they're objective on a sense (to that level).<br /> <br /> For instsance, you could argue a &quot;lack of knowledge&quot; in the sense that the rules &quot;exist on society&quot; even if one member doesn't follow them (Say, &quot;a kid doesn't know hurting a bug it's bad&quot;). That's a level of objectivity above of &quot;pure subjectivity&quot;, but it's still very much within the idea that we (humans) make morality (and thus can and do change the rules).<br /> <br /> I guess you could also posit that rules are subjective but not to humans/mages. However, in that case, from the pov of humans/mages they woud be pretty much objective. <hr /><br /> I've tought an example that could help:<br /> <br /> Let's say that a benevolent mage of the Celestial Chorus arrives at a small village where he's welcomed because he heals people and stuff. But, the thing is, it's a village of cannibals. The chorister isn't a fan of this, so he's going to cast a Mind spell to change everyone into believing cannibalism it's not ok and they shouldn't do this anymore (well, cannibalism it's not ok, and they shouldn't do this anymore, they just fail at seeing it because of age old tradition, but people can change trough empathy - even w/o magick - so why not?. Or so believes the mage), the Focus it's going to be &quot;preaching&quot; (about the One and community of all, love, life, and such)<br /> <br /> What could one do as an ST?<br /> <br /> I guess you could disallow this on the grounds morality it's a force that can't be changed by magick?. But I doubt even the proponents of &quot;morality as something Magick can't change&quot; are going to be so extreme. Rather I bet they would say, well, that you can magically alter their opinions about wheter cannibalism it's bad or not, but<i> cannibalism being bad or not isn't going to change</i><br /> <br /> However, note that this imples cannibalism can be bad or not independant of those opinions. As for <i>what kind of existence morailty has</i> in that case, I will let the burden of proof be on the side that posits such a thing.<br /> <br /> Even then, some choices have to be made, and where you stand would probably change them. I see these choices<br /> <br /> 1)<br /> You could allow it but it's Always Vulgar, it's <i>never </i>going to be permanent, it's brainwashing and going to leave brainwashing Ressonance on you, and it's going to kick your dog -<br /> <br /> I would call that the &quot;morality it's something that Magick <b><i>shouldn't be able to</i></b> <b><i>change</i></b>&quot;. This way mages can't do this at a large scale ever. You could argue it's pushing against the laws of the world, the Cosmological Constant. <br /> <br /> 2)<br /> You could allow it, it's Coincident and instant if done little by little over a long period of time because people has historically changed their minds if engeged by a charismatic and beloved orators. Heck, maybe they weren't such a fan of cannibalism after all<br /> <br /> Wheter morality exists as an objective thing outside it's immaterial to this choice (as above, one could say you're not &quot;changing morality&quot;, just the &quot;moral code of one society&quot;). But at least morality on an intersubjective/societal/cultural level CAN be changed<br /> <br /> So. Why don't mages do this more often to? Well, first changing morality isn't exactly the same as changing the ontological beliefs that make magick work... But, mostly, nobody changed what it's &quot;bad&quot; and &quot;good&quot; at a global scale to &quot;win the war&quot; because global magicks to change everyone's minds require too many successes, too much effort, and are prone to catastrophical failures even before everyone with &quot;detect magick&quot; powers come to your doorstep asking what the big, all encompassing, magickal nuke it's about.<br /> <br /> 3)<br /> A middle ground of sorts - Like, maybe it's Coincidental but can't be made permanent. Or it's Vulgar, can be made permanent, but it's always magical and thus can be perceived and unweaved<br /> <br /> Again, I could see several ways this could be spinned. Morality has at least some level of resistance to change that would make it at least very, very hard. but that doesn't need to mean it exists as an independant thing. Maybe it's just SO tied to the Consensus that you need to change it naturally, and if you use Spheres it's unnatural. Maybe it's tied to some Foundation (like how planes were canonically hard to invent, and had to tie in other Foundations), maybe you need to change the Zone before really starting to &quot;convert people&quot; on a more casual fashion <hr /><br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Heck, it's entirely possible that the reason why nobody has ever cast a spell to change morality is because there <i>is </i>no such thing as morality to change. But I'm not sure I'd ascribe <i>that </i>view even to the <i>Nephandi. </i>Not as something that they actually <i>believe.</i> </div> </div> </div>Or maybe there it's no such thing to change in the sense morality doesn't exist as an independant - non abstract - thing. I could see a lot of mages thinking this. You can't change &quot;morality&quot; outside of &quot;society&quot;, but if you can change society you can change morality. If your own morals don't exist outside your beliefs, you don't change them with magick mostly because you don't need to, not because you couldn't<br /> <br /> The thing about we not &quot;knowing what morality is&quot; it's that then we can't say that it is something that Magick can't change. It's like saying &quot;magick can't change X. What it's X?. I don't know....&quot;<br /> Well, when you know and can define what morality IS, we can talk about if magick can change it or not","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 05-14-2024, 12:35 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-14T10:40:01-07:00","id":1535571,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1535107-awakened-ghouls?p=1535569#post1535569\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Let's say that a benevolent mage of the Celestial Chorus arrives at a small village where he's welcomed because he heals people and stuff. But, the thing is, it's a village of cannibals. The chorister isn't a fan of this, so he's going to cast a Mind spell to change everyone into believing cannibalism it's not ok and they shouldn't do this anymore (well, cannibalism it's not ok, and they shouldn't do this anymore, they just fail at seeing it because of age old tradition, but people can change trough empathy - even w/o magick - so why not?. Or so believes the mage), the Focus it's going to be &quot;preaching&quot; (about the One and community of all, love, life, and such)</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> The first problem here is that the Chorister already knows he is right and the cannibals are wrong. Full stop. The cannibals might disagree but that doesn't matter because they are wrong. So he's not trying to change morality, he's trying to get them to see what reality is. His spell, then, is not designed to change morality, just to change their minds. Even if he could magically change morality, that's not what he's trying to do.<br /> <br /> Changing the consensus by magical mind control (which I do not allow for thematic and power reasons, by the way) is not the same thing as direct spell casting. Changing the consensus so that rocks are magnetic is not the same thing as magically altering the rocks themselves to be magnetic. Changing the consensus opinion on morality is not the same thing as magically altering morality.<br /> <br /> Which gets to a real problem with magically changing morality. In order to do so, a mage would have to recognize that there is a moral reality, that there is a difference between his moral reality and the world's moral reality, that the world's moral reality is the preeminent one (otherwise why would he need to change it), and that his own moral reality is the preeminent one (otherwise why would he have the right to change the world's). That is a very strange headspace for a mage to hold and not one that lends itself to successful casting. <br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-14T13:52:34-07:00","id":1535589,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1535107-awakened-ghouls?p=1535571#post1535571\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The first problem here is that the Chorister already knows he is right and the cannibals are wrong. Full stop. The cannibals might disagree but that doesn't matter because they are wrong. So he's not trying to change morality, he's trying to get them to see what reality is. His spell, then, is not designed to change morality, just to change their minds. Even if he could magically change morality, that's not what he's trying to do.</div> </div> </div> </div>I never said the Chorister knows. I said he believes it to be so. The example posits us as an impartial third party that it's free to judge if morality it's being changed or not. If morality it's a thing at all, then we should be able to judge if an Effect it's changing it.<br /> <br /> And if we judge that the moral code of a group of people has been changed, and that that's the same as morality changing because morality it's cultural ... wether the Chorister thinks morality comes from God and thus he wasn't intending to change it it's immaterial. At that point it's like a Dreamspeaker intending to summon a spirit and ending with an EDE in it's place. It's just a wordplay<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Changing the consensus by magical mind control (which I do not allow for thematic and power reasons, by the way) is not the same thing as direct spell casting. Changing the consensus so that rocks are magnetic is not the same thing as magically altering the rocks themselves to be magnetic. Changing the consensus opinion on morality is not the same thing as magically altering morality. </div> </div> </div>Rocks are pretty objective things. The difference between rocks and morals on most subjectivist perspectives it's that morals are part of culture. Culture has stuff to say about rocks, but rocks aren't part of it on the same way morals are. Most moral subjectivists don't believe rocks are entirely a cultural construct<br /> <br /> Granted, from a very extreme perspective, it <i>could </i>be on Mage that rocks <i>are</i> a social construct (a perspective which I don't endorse)<br /> <br /> Now, If morality it's part of culture, just like you can change part of the rock if you can change the rock, you can change morality if you can change culture. That would be the point. If morality it's part of the Consensus, and changing the Consensus isn't impossible, then you can <i>directly </i>change morality as part of it.<br /> <br /> BUT I would preffer not to mention the Consensus itself as the &quot;target&quot; of the spell (I mentoned it as a possible &quot;complication&quot;, not as target) because the Consensus it's it's own can of worms. Spells can have AoE. Mind can have AoE (it has rules for it). Should we forbide AoR to target a RZ, even if the RZ it's really small because &quot;o my Consensus&quot;? Maybe the Consensus it's &quot;below&quot; both morals and the culture, as an underlying force that would jumpscare your spell if you sh things too far, or too fast<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Which gets to a real problem with magically changing morality. In order to do so, a mage would have to recognize that there is a moral reality, that there is a difference between his moral reality and the world's moral reality, that the world's moral reality is the preeminent one (otherwise why would he need to change it), and that his own moral reality is the preeminent one (otherwise why would he have the right to change the world's). That is a very strange headspace for a mage to hold and not one that lends itself to successful casting. </div> </div> </div>You just need to change one extremely unfair and uncalled for premise for another: change &quot;there's a moral reality of the world&quot; by &quot;there are multiple moral realities on the world, that clash and figth between themselves&quot;.<br /> That being the case:There's a difference between moral realities everywhere, some are preeminent on some places, your own it's *not* the preeminent one in this place but you hold it because you have free will and maybe you believe yours it's better.Or maybe it's just better for you and the people you care about - not for everyone, but it's still important enough that you're going to figth for it (or , heck, maybe it's just better for yourself but &quot;migth makes it rigth&quot; - it's not like mages are all nice people)<br /> <br /> But I really don't think you need to do all of that. I was mostly answering at the idea that morality it's immune to change and exists above the cosmos. Not at &quot;targeting morals&quot; directly. If you can do that indirectly, and have reasons to do so, then you can change morals.<br /> <br /> And if you can't target morals directly because they're just a sociological concept, sepparate from their actual reality as a moving force on society. Is then really fair to say that magick can't alter them?: Like, you could say that the Chorister didn't change the morals of the cannibals because that moral system it's still part of their cultural legacy (viewed as a historian, those morals &quot;existed&quot; - viewed as a sociologist, they could even exist elsewhere). But that's little better than to say morals are an abstraction that doesn't have any reality as a concrete thing that magick could target.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 05-14-2024, 05:00 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-14T14:59:00-07:00","id":1535591,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1535107-awakened-ghouls?p=1535589#post1535589\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I never said the Chorister knows. I said he believes it to be so. The example posits us as an impartial third party that it's free to judge if morality it's being changed or not. If morality it's a thing at all, then we should be able to judge if an Effect it's changing it.</div> </div> </div> </div>You are getting hung up on whether he can actually know, but my point was that he is certain that he does know. Because he already considers it to be right, he's not going to try to change it to be right, he's just going to work to change their minds so that they can know the truth. That's his motivation. Which means that even in the aforementioned 3rd case, he's not changing morality because he's not even trying to.<br /> <br /> That matters because there are really three main approaches I can see that could be true in Mage.<br /> <br /> 1) Morality is objective<br /> 2) Morality comes from cultural belief and has no real existence outside of cultural belief<br /> 3) Morality comes from cultural belief and because this is Mage, that gives it a real existence outside of cultural belief.<br /> <br /> In the first case there is no spell to directly change morality because it can't be magically changed. It doesn't matter whether the Chorister is right, though he does happen to be right.<br /> <br /> In the second case there is no spell to directly change morality because there is nothing to target. Morality is solely a function of minds and can only be affected through minds, not directly. As it turns out the Chorister is wrong, but even if he knew the truth he still couldn't directly affect morality.<br /> <br /> The third case is the only one where morality has a coherent presence and is alterable. This is where it matters that the Chorister already believes himself to be right because he isn't even trying to directly alter morality. At best he is indirectly altering it. He's not altering morality itself, he's tipping over the first domino that will lead to it changing.<br /> <br /> And even then he is only doing so if magically mind controlling someone changes their impact on the Consensus (which I don't allow).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You just need to change one extremely unfair and uncalled for premise for another: change &quot;there's a moral reality of the world&quot; by &quot;there are multiple moral realities on the world, that clash and figth between themselves&quot;. </div> </div> </div>Say what? The example you quoted had two competing moral realities: the one in reality and the one in the Chorister's head. That was my point. Even giving the optimal conditions to let mages change morality directly, it still takes a very strange headspace to cast.<br /> <br /> In order to try and cast on morality itself a mage needs to recognize it as in some way real and important. He has to consider that it both supercedes his own opinions on right and wrong, but is at the same time inferior to his opinions. If it doesn't do the former he has no need to change it, except maybe as a parlor trick no one will notice. If he doesn't do the latter, he can't actually intend to change it at all.<br /> \u200b","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-14T15:44:35-07:00","id":1535594,"title":"","content":"Due to some editing I mentioned the third case before saying what it was. Sorry for any confusion","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-14T16:56:35-07:00","id":1535597,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1535107-awakened-ghouls?p=1535591#post1535591\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">he already considers it to be right, he's not going to try to change it to be right, he's just going to work to change their minds so that they can know the truth.</div> </div> </div> </div>As I see it, this is not very different from changing their morals, at least on cases 2 and 3.<br /> <br /> If moral has no real existence outside of cultural belief, then I would think that to &quot;change their minds&quot; it's functionally the same as changing their morals. Beliefs can be targeted, <i>and if those are all that there's to be said about moral reality ..</i>. then why it's changing the cultural beliefs not changing - directly even - morality?<br /> <br /> If comes from cultural belief and because this is Mage, that gives it a real existence outside of cultural belief, then it could have some complications. But it will depend on what sort of reality it takes. I never said anything about changing the Consensus, I only mentioned it as a <i>complication</i> that could be invoqued to make it harder. One of many different complications.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> That's his motivation. Which means that even in the aforementioned 3rd case, he's not changing morality because he's not even trying to. </div> </div> </div>The example had the intended purpose of serving to ilustrate something that COULD (depending on how you see things) be framed as trying to change the shared morality of a group of people. This is why I don't think that talking about &quot;intent&quot; matters a lot. It seems like nitpicking &quot;he wouldn't because Focus wouldn't allow it&quot;, Well, it doesn't matter, we as a 3rd party could interpret it as functionally changing morality anyway, can't we?. and if it's so problematic just change the mage, I chose a chorister with that intent because it seemed appropiate but surely you can be offended by cannibalism as many different types of philosopy, choose one that would be able to conceptualize this. If the problem it's that it's &quot;impossible to conceptualize as a mage&quot;, well, I don't think it is and I explain why below (and also above but it was flaggued because I always think about better ways to say the things way too late)<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Say what? The example you quoted had two competing moral realities: the one in reality and the one in the Chorister's head. That was my point. Even giving the optimal conditions to let mages change morality directly, it still takes a very strange headspace to cast. </div> </div> </div>Well, I never established a &quot;morality of the world&quot;. Also I don't think that you need to do all those steps to get the result.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> In order to try and cast on morality itself a mage needs to recognize it as in some way real and important. </div> </div> </div>You can recognize morality as something in some way real and important even if it's indistinguishable from the humble &quot;moral feelings&quot; of the individual. That's why there's so much philosophy about these even among moral relativists.<br /> <br /> But, of course, if you think that all there's to be said about morality it's cultural, then a consequence would be that a change of the morality it's a cultural change (it can be said to be equivalent to a cultural change).That doesn't mean it's not important, it could be a matter of life and death for you, because morality lends itself to action<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> He has to consider that it both supercedes his own opinions on right and wrong, but is at the same time inferior to his opinions. </div> </div> </div>It could &quot;supercede your opinions&quot; on the sense that it's the dominant paradigm, it's stronger, but it's not better. That there are more than one morality, and one needs to go, doesn't mean that morals are all equal, or simultaneusly bad and good (altrough they could be if it's on different senses, or at different times)<br /> <br /> Think about how people figth to change a systematic issue they believe it's unjust: They're tipically demonized and vilified because they're going against what's common sense. But they figth to change these opinions, very often by force. You could say they're &quot;on the moral side&quot;, thay would certainly say so<br /> <br /> ... but it's so outlandish if you say that &quot;morality changed&quot; if they win and common sense about morality did changed?.<br /> <br /> If the answer it's no, then you can conceptualize yourself trying to change the morals of the people. Many would say that they're &quot;helping them to see the light&quot;, but at this point that's just wordplay, isn't it?, you could equally say, well, they challenged and changed the old moral system<br /> <br /> Bottom line: I think you can have a very clear intent. Sure, you're representing what's &quot;trully moral&quot; all day, but as long as you're charitable enough to aknowledge the older code was a morality of sorts, altrough you don't agree with some of it, and want things to change, you can say the words yourself (If you don't, I can still say them as an observer. But I don't understand why it should be so hard to conceptualize). The target was more dobtful, maybe it's there maybe it isn't, maybe you can affect the thing indirectly.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 05-14-2024, 08:26 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-14T17:25:11-07:00","id":1535601,"title":"","content":"One thought I just had.<br /> <br /> Do we have an example, any at all, of Magick targeting directly an emerging phenomenon?<br /> <br /> If we cast Morality as something that exists as a result of social consensus, that's just one example of an emerging phenomenon. Systems in general, both human or physical, are full of emerging phenomena.<br /> <br /> The decision-making of businesses and organizations, as an example, depends on an &quot;Org Mind&quot; that is an emerging phenomenon from the internal systems and relationships within, not a single decision of any particular person (even if it looks like), but at the same time recognizable as a mind of sorts (frequently with recognizable personality).<br /> <br /> Can a Mage target those literal emerging patterns as an actual Pattern?<br /> <br /> <br /> And just as a curiosity: cannibalism is still legal in some places because of a thing called funerary cannibalism. For some people this is the right way of paying respect to the deceased.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-14T17:46:54-07:00","id":1535603,"title":"","content":"Would you consider the Digital Web to be an emergent phenomenon? If so, the Virtual Adepts are <i>all over </i>the concept of using Magick to affect emergent phenomena. There's also the economy, which the Syndicate influences using a variation of Prime.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-14T19:28:50-07:00","id":1535605,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1535107-awakened-ghouls?p=1535603#post1535603\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Would you consider the Digital Web to be an emergent phenomenon? If so, the Virtual Adepts are <i>all over </i>the concept of using Magick to affect emergent phenomena. There's also the economy, which the Syndicate influences using a variation of Prime.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm not sure.<br /> <br /> The Digital Web is a realm created by an emergent phenomenon more than such a phenomenon itself. I was thinking about something that remains abstract in nature.<br /> <br /> Economy is a more interesting idea, but all Procedures I ever read from the Syndicate use it as a medium for the Procedure itself, not the target of the effect. In fact, we only ever hear about their procedures <b>on</b> the economy when we're reading about how they're secretly the cause of the latest crisis due to Market Correction.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-15T08:33:38-07:00","id":1535641,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1535107-awakened-ghouls?p=1535597#post1535597\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> If moral has no real existence outside of cultural belief, then I would think that to &quot;change their minds&quot; it's functionally the same as changing their morals. Beliefs can be targeted, <i>and if those are all that there's to be said about moral reality ..</i>. then why it's changing the cultural beliefs not changing - directly even - morality?</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes, it is the same as changing <i>their</i> morals. Changing morality itself, though, becomes impossible because there's nothing a mage can point to as morality itself. It has no existence of its own to change. At this point the issue largely devolves into semantics. You can say that counts as targeting morality, but its not any more accurate as saying there's nothing concrete to target.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> If comes from cultural belief and because this is Mage, that gives it a real existence outside of cultural belief, then it could have some complications. But it will depend on what sort of reality it takes. I never said anything about changing the Consensus, I only mentioned it as a <i>complication</i> that could be invoqued to make it harder. One of many different complications. </div> </div> </div>If morality has an existence outside of cultural belief because of Mage's consensus metaphysics, that means that targeting opinions on morality is not the same thing as targeting morality. That's why I say that in case 3, changing minds only indirectly changes morality, and if it does it does so through the consensus.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The example had the intended purpose of serving to ilustrate something that COULD (depending on how you see things) be framed as trying to change the shared morality of a group of people. This is why I don't think that talking about &quot;intent&quot; matters a lot. It seems like nitpicking &quot;he wouldn't because Focus wouldn't allow it&quot;, Well, it doesn't matter, we as a 3rd party could interpret it as functionally changing morality anyway, can't we?. and if it's so problematic just change the mage, I chose a chorister with that intent because it seemed appropiate but surely you can be offended by cannibalism as many different types of philosopy, choose one that would be able to conceptualize this. If the problem it's that it's &quot;impossible to conceptualize as a mage&quot;, well, I don't think it is and I explain why below (and also above but it was flaggued because I always think about better ways to say the things way too late) </div> </div> </div>This isn't about focus or paradigm. In the interests of reasoning this out I've been assuming the Chorister's paradigm is open enough that he can do anything he wants to. His intent matters because it determines what he is even trying to do, and what he is trying to do is change minds, not morality itself. Even if there is a local morality itself that he could target, and even if he is paradigmatically capable of targeting it, that isn't what he's trying to do here.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You can recognize morality as something in some way real and important even if it's indistinguishable from the humble &quot;moral feelings&quot; of the individual. That's why there's so much philosophy about these even among moral relativists. </div> </div> </div>You can recognize that other people's opinions on right and wrong have an effect on you. That isn't the same thing as recognizing that their moral code has a claim on your behavior. To go back to the earlier, simpler way I stated the problem, many mages can say:<br /> <br /> &quot;Currently you think I am morally wrong, but after I cast this spell you will think I am morally right.&quot;<br /> <br /> But nobody says:<br /> <br /> &quot;Currently I am morally wrong, but after I cast this spell I will be morally right.&quot;<br /> <br /> Mages don't actually behave like morality is something they can change.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-15T15:06:25-07:00","id":1535680,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yes, it is the same as changing <i>their</i> morals. Changing morality itself, though, becomes impossible because there's nothing a mage can point to as morality itself. It has no existence of its own to change. At this point the issue largely devolves into semantics. You can say that counts as targeting morality, but its not any more accurate as saying there's nothing concrete to target.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yep. And I've already had said as much.<br /> <br /> However, I think you're just as faulty at wordplay here: We're talking about morality as something of a special reality magick can't target. If it's nothing then it isn't &quot;something&quot;. A better way to put it it's that morality, that way understood, it's an intelectual abstraction. And Magick can't target abstractions directly, <i>any </i>abstraction (not just morals). It's not that morality it's special in any way:<br /> <br /> I could say <b>chairs can't be targeted by magick</b>, because you can change A chair, you could even eradicate the whole capacity of humanity to produce and employ chairs, and still &quot;chairs themselves&quot; are still possible. If somebody had resisted your efforts and had a chair, people could point and say you didn't <i>really </i>changed what chairs are.<br /> <br /> Still, saying that chairs are somehow a reality that magick can't target would be silly. In the same way, I think that it's silly to say that magick can't change morality if morality it's understood as a social construct. All the &quot;living&quot;, meaningfull, aspects of morality are being changed. A historian would watch the tribe and reccord that there was a change of the moral code of that tribe.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> If morality has an existence outside of cultural belief because of Mage's consensus metaphysics, that means that targeting opinions on morality is not the same thing as targeting morality. That's why I say that in case 3, changing minds only indirectly changes morality, and if it does it does so through the consensus. </div> </div> </div>OH...if you understand it that way, thenyeah. Of course. It would be aking to wat targeting the &quot;spirit&quot; on the Umbra it's for werewolf (on Mage it was never very clear if that changes anything or not)<br /> <br /> But 3 was more of an commodin for different posibilities. Depending on how you understand 3, changing morality directly could be almost as impossible for magick as on 1 (if moral reality it's related to the Foundations, then you can't change it with magick, not even if you could change the Consensus - all you can do it's try to slowly play within the &quot;moral rules&quot; to undermine some and empower others).<br /> On the other hand, I wasn't of the intention of excluding morality as &quot;cultural belief&quot;, moral reality (as something pertaining with the beliefs of the people) could be backed by the Consensus (Like how a spell could be targeted by Unbelief if it swings hard against the Consensus). That would make changing it a lot harder, and perhaps it would be impossible to do it with magick alone (like how you can't change a Reality Zone with magick alone), but not nearly as impossible as other alternatives<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> His intent matters because it determines what he is even trying to do, and what he is trying to do is change minds, not morality itself. Even if there is a local morality itself that he could target, and even if he is paradigmatically capable of targeting it, that isn't what he's trying to do here. </div> </div> </div>But what he's trying to do could be judged differently from an outsiders perspective. In this case, the chorister an I just disagree on what Morality is. That's he's doing the casting doesn't mean that he has to be more rigth about what the Effect it's doing. However, I do believe after saying that thing you're quoting that I do owe an example where the intent does agree with the words:<br /> <br /> Let's say that a benevolent mage of the NWO arrives at a small village where he's welcomed because he protects people and stuff. But, the thing is, it's a village of cannibals. The Agent knows that this ain't very mutual and could cause problems to the tribe as they intersect with the modern world, so he's going to develop a procedure to alter the supersctructural composition their moral system, changing some tennets by others that are more mutual<br /> <br /> To be clear: The mage it's explicitly intending to change &quot;morality&quot;, as something he believes exist as a structural charactersistic of cultures<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">To go back to the earlier, simpler way I stated the problem, many mages can say:<br /> <br /> &quot;Currently you think I am morally wrong, but after I cast this spell you will think I am morally right.&quot;<br /> <br /> But nobody says:<br /> <br /> &quot;Currently I am morally wrong, but after I cast this spell I will be morally right.&quot;<br /> <br /> Mages don't actually behave like morality is something they can change.</div> </div> </div> </div>To go back to my 1rst post on the thread:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">I disagree with the idea that mage posits moral as something above change<br /> <br /> A completely opposite way of viewing this, and at least equally valid, it's that morality could be little more than an opinion (an intersubjective valuation) that has no real existence outside of &quot;belief&quot;. Thus mages don't change it not because they can't but because it would be pointless: Mages don't cast spells to change their opinion about anything , not just about moral things. There's no magick in the books to change your mind about anything, moral or otherwise<br /> <br /> Let's say that you want to take a walk: You can go trough road A or road B. Neither it's a particularly moral or immoral choice.<br /> <br /> How do you alter your choise between A or B trough magick?. You could scan the paths to see what one it's better, but that's not directly &quot;altering your choice&quot;, rather it's providing you with reasons to change your own opinion.<br /> <br /> How do you change &quot;what you want to do&quot; with magick?. You don't, because the moment you want to do &quot;this&quot;, you've already changed your mind <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> ...<br /> <br /> <br /> Similarly you can &quot;change your mind&quot; with magick while resisting an external influence. I.E:: Probably a bonded ghoul-mage believes that his domitor it's a great person, if not always a good one. Love often changes people's wordviews, including morality. It could be that the mage now sees something (like, say, the importance of hierarchy or nobility in the case of Ventrue) in a different ligth compared to before the Bonding, and copious positive interactions with &quot;the loved one&quot;. But after a moment of abuse the mage could change his own mind a little, enough to cast a spell to brek &quot;the bond&quot; maybe - and then I imagine the vampire, and the actions the mage realized for it, are going to be seen in a very different ligth. A 100% tunarround of worldview about some beliefs that would be important for both of you it's not something I would discard<br /> <br /> &quot;But you're not directly changing your worldview with magick&quot;. No, because it would be pointless, the moment you want to do that it's already changed. But you're still totally changing your morality with magick, and you're resisting it changing away again when the effect of the bond resumes. To not see these changes as &quot;magical&quot; would be pushing it. No?. Even if you're not directly casting &quot;I will change my own opinions&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>So: &quot;Currently I am morally wrong, but after I cast this spell I will be morally right.&quot;<br /> <br /> Yes, I agree mages don't do that<br /> <br /> My point was that morality doesn't<i> need </i>to be a <i>something </i>outside the influence of magick. This is true wheter morality can be changed, wheter it can be changed partially or indirectly, or wheter &quot;morality itself&quot; can't be changed because there's never a reality to change - the latter it's an <i>immaterial </i>objection to my point (never said better)<br /> <br /> For that purpose I proposed theories of morality that do aknowledge it as a real thing, but not as something outside what could be under the influence of magick on some capacity (perhaps not on every capacity - it doesn't matter)<br /> <br /> So, you don't think those very real things are &quot;morality itself&quot;, so the examples don't count as changing morality, even if you can aknowledge &quot;morality itself&quot; could lack reality?<br /> <br /> At that point it IS just a disagreement on what can and can't be called &quot;morality&quot;.<br /> <br /> But if you were to see the things from my perspective, my &quot;wordplay&quot;, then mages do behave like morality is something they can change. They definitely do that when they change the morality of other people. They protect their own from external change.<br /> <br /> They don't alter morals to be morally rigth on their own mind, that WOULD require a very strange mentality like what you described, but it's also kind of a strawman: Mages that have beliefs that oppose the changing of morals of course wouldn't be able to do so, but if it's subjective and can change then you wouldn't do so because you wouldn't ever need or want to - none of these two talk about the impossibility of altering morality (magically or otherwise), just that there would be no occasion, And that only for a very restrictive (and a little absurd) example","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 05-15-2024, 06:38 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-15T15:47:24-07:00","id":1535686,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1535107-awakened-ghouls?p=1535601#post1535601\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">One thought I just had.<br /> <br /> Do we have an example, any at all, of Magick targeting directly an emerging phenomenon?<br /> <br /> If we cast Morality as something that exists as a result of social consensus, that's just one example of an emerging phenomenon. Systems in general, both human or physical, are full of emerging phenomena.<br /> <br /> The decision-making of businesses and organizations, as an example, depends on an &quot;Org Mind&quot; that is an emerging phenomenon from the internal systems and relationships within, not a single decision of any particular person (even if it looks like), but at the same time recognizable as a mind of sorts (frequently with recognizable personality).<br /> <br /> Can a Mage target those literal emerging patterns as an actual Pattern?</div> </div> </div> </div>I would think so, unless I'm misunderstanding some concept here<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>M20, Entropy 5:</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">The most esoteric applications of Entropy allow the mage to alter ideas, strengthening or breaking down concepts. Although he does not affect the actual workings of<br /> consciousness, he can cause synapses to misfire (thus confusing perceptions and mental processing, inflicting penalties on an enemy\u2019s dice rolls), bind someone to an oath, or degrade the patterns of thought. By doing so, that Entropic Master can craft, perpetrate, reinforce, and undermine arguments, beliefs, and even memories.<br /> <br /> ...<br /> <br /> By offering compelling statements, the Master can create or destroy memes (see p. 594), thus influencing whole patterns of belief ...</div> </div> </div> </div>Arguably, since it<i> can't </i>alter the things directly, it can <i>only </i>affect emerging phenomenons, and <i>only </i>as emerging phenomenons<br /> <br /> Oh, but if these are things that have to remain abstract, then I guess only &quot;patterns of belief&quot; count - altrough this is indirect, trough memes. <i>Maybe </i>also concepts and ideas? (Not so if they're only the concepts as they exist on an individual mind, I think it can be read both ways, altrough it's a weird use of the word &quot;concept&quot;, it could be &quot;the target's concepts&quot; what you're affecting)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 05-15-2024, 06:58 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-16T09:10:02-07:00","id":1535736,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1535107-awakened-ghouls?p=1535680#post1535680\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> My point was that morality doesn't<i> need </i>to be a <i>something </i>outside the influence of magick. This is true wheter morality can be changed, wheter it can be changed partially or indirectly, or wheter &quot;morality itself&quot; can't be changed because there's never a reality to change - the latter it's an <i>immaterial </i>objection to my point (never said better)<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>I certainly get where you are coming from now. I should point out that my position is not that morality <i>has</i> to be beyond the reach of magic, but that it appears to be functionally beyond the reach of magic. As <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar421_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"421\" data-vbnamecard=\"421\">Dataweaver</a> pointed out, there's never been a published effect that affected morality itself. We could take this to mean that it is actually impossible.<br /> <br /> However, my point is that it doesn't matter whether it is actually impossible or not because is either impossible, or functionally impossible, or functionally meaningless. Even if morality itself can be targeted, the approach a mage would have to take to alter it is extremely niche. In Case 1 morality can't be changed, in Case 2 it can't be changed <i>in that way</i>, and in Case 3 it is theoretically possible but requires a mentality that goes against how mages cast spells.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}